            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "I don’t have live news access, so I can’t verify the very latest updates beyond my knowledge cutoff. As of the most recent information I have:\n\n- NASA’s **Perseverance rover is still operating in Jezero Crater** on Mars, studying ancient lake and river-delta environments for signs of past habitability.\n- It has continued collecting and caching **rock-core samples** intended for eventual return to Earth by a future **Mars Sample Return** mission.\n- In 2024, Perseverance was exploring areas associated with the ancient river system that once flowed into Jezero, including terrain near **Neretva Vallis** and the crater’s western fan/margin region.\n- Its companion helicopter, **Ingenuity**, ended its mission in January 2024 after damage to its rotor blades, but it completed 72 flights—far beyond its original technology-demonstration goal.\n- NASA has been reassessing the Mars Sample Return architecture because of cost and schedule concerns, while Perseverance continues its science campaign.\n\nFor the newest official updates, check NASA/JPL’s Perseverance mission page or NASA’s Mars newsroom.",
                "annotations": [],
                "logprobs": []
              }
